=== SCHEMA ===
migrations/000169_positions_si.up.sql defines:

  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS positions CASCADE;

  CREATE TABLE positions (
    vehicle_id    BIGINT NOT NULL,
    ts            TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
    lat           DOUBLE PRECISION NOT NULL,
    lng           DOUBLE PRECISION NOT NULL,
    altitude_m    DOUBLE PRECISION,
    speed_mps     DOUBLE PRECISION,
    heading_deg   DOUBLE PRECISION,
    gps_state     TEXT,
    odometer_m    DOUBLE PRECISION,
    est_range_m   DOUBLE PRECISION,
    rated_range_m DOUBLE PRECISION,
    ideal_range_m DOUBLE PRECISION,
    PRIMARY KEY (vehicle_id, ts)
  );

  SELECT create_hypertable('positions', 'ts',
      chunk_time_interval => INTERVAL '7 days',
      if_not_exists => TRUE);

  CREATE INDEX positions_vehicle_ts ON positions (vehicle_id, ts DESC);
  CREATE INDEX positions_ts          ON positions (ts DESC);

  COMMENT ON TABLE  positions       ...
  COMMENT ON COLUMN positions.<col> ...   (one per non-PK column)

WHY DROP TABLE IF EXISTS at the top:
  The pre-phase-42 positions table created in 000142_baseline_typed
  (line 281) uses wire-format columns (latitude, longitude, heading,
  elevation_m, speed_mph) with a FOREIGN KEY to vehicles(id) and
  TimescaleDB compression + retention policies. Phase-42 is forward-
  only with no legacy retention (.github/ARCHITECTURE.md ADR-004), so
  this migration drops the legacy table wholesale and rebuilds with
  SI-canonical columns. CASCADE is safe — a grep over migrations/
  found zero `REFERENCES positions(...)` clauses, so no other table
  depends on positions's PK.

WHY column-name suffix carries the unit:
  ADR-004 #4 mandates that every unit-bearing column lives in
  canonical SI and that the unit suffix is part of the column name.
  This makes the SI contract self-documenting and audit-grep-able
  (a `git grep '_mph\|_miles\|altitude_ft\|range_mi'` over the
  schema can never produce a false positive).

WHY heading_deg has no SI suffix:
  Compass heading is angular, not unit-bearing in the SI sense; the
  prompt explicitly excludes it from SI conversion. The `_deg` suffix
  documents that the column stores degrees [0, 360), not radians.

migrations/000169_positions_si.down.sql:
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS positions CASCADE;
  (Forward-only rollback — there is no legacy schema to restore.)

